body:
This logic looks off. Both checks have `p_range.end.line <= end.line`, and `start.line <= p_range.start.line` is always true if `start.line <= p_range.end.line` is true, so this is effectively just the second condition. The case where `p_range.start_line <= end.line < p_range.end.line` overlaps but this misses it.

So it should be `start.line <= p_range.end.line && end.line >= p_range.start.line;`.
After looking it up to verify, here is another way to think about it. It doesn't overlap when `(start.line > p_range.end.line || end.line < p_range.start.line)` which can be inverted. Assuming start <= end.

And this only considers line and not the character, which is otherwise a part of range, so maybe it should be called `line_overlaps`. Assuming you don't care about the character here.

```suggestion
	bool overlaps_line(const Range &p_range) const {
		return start.line <= p_range.end.line && end.line >= p_range.start.line;
	}
```

Or if you do care about characters, it should be something like:
```cpp
if (start.line > p_range.end.line || end.line < p_range.start.line) {
	return false;
}
if (start.line == p_range.end.line) {
	return start.character <= p_range.end.character;
}
if (end.line == p_range.start.line) {
	return end.character >= p_range.start.character;
}
return true;
```

